<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hayashi Razan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1583–1657</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hayashi Razan was a Japanese Neo-Confucian scholar who served the first four Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> as adviser and drafter of documents. A follower of the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, he promoted a philosophy of hierarchical social order and loyalty that suited the new regime. The private academy he founded in Edo in 1630 became, under his descendants, the official shogunal school, the Shōheikō.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan.jpg" alt="Hayashi Razan" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9E%97%E7%BE%85%E5%B1%B1_%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
